{"name":"Apple Turnover","hex":"#e8c194","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"apple turnover","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pastry dessert filled with pieces of sweetened apples, with the pastry folded over to enclose the filling."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apple_turnover"}],"entries":[{"title":"Turnover (food)","blocks":[{"text":"A turnover is a baked or fried food, made by placing a filling on a piece of pastry or viennoiserie dough. The dough is then folded over and sealed, and is then cooked by either baking or frying. Turnovers can be sweet or savoury and are often eaten as a sort of portable meal or dessert. Throughout the world, turnovers are known by different names. For example, in Spanish-speaking countries they are known as empanadas, while pasty, originally a Cornish term, has spread across the globe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/M%C3%B6nichwald_-_Krapfenkirtag_-_Schmerkrapfen.jpg/330px-M%C3%B6nichwald_-_Krapfenkirtag_-_Schmerkrapfen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turnover_(food)","wikidata":"Q10785085"},{"title":"Apple","blocks":[{"text":"An apple is the round, edible fruit of an apple tree. Fruit trees of the orchard or domestic apple, the most widely grown in the genus, are cultivated worldwide. The tree originated in Central Asia, where its wild ancestor, Malus sieversii, is still found. Apples have been grown for thousands of years in Eurasia before they were introduced to the American continent by European colonists. Apples have cultural significance in many mythologies and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple","wikidata":"Q89"}],"images":[{"srcset":"